# Strategic Defect Engineering of Metal–Organic Frameworks for Optimizing the Fabrication of Single‐Atom Catalysts.

## abstract
Single‐atom catalysts (SACs) have garnered enormous interest due to their remarkable catalysis activity. However, the exploitation of universal synthesis strategy and regulation of coordination environment of SACs remain a great challenge. Herein, a versatile synthetic strategy is demonstrated to generate a series of transition metal SACs (M SAs/NC, M = Co, Cu, Mn; NC represents the nitrogen‐doped carbon) through defect engineering of metal‐organic frameworks (MOFs). The interatomic distance between metal sites can be increased by deliberately introducing structural defects within the MOF framework, which inhibits metal aggregation and consequently results in an approximately 70% increase in single metal atom yield. Additionally, the coordination structures of metal sites can also be facilely tuned. The optimized Co SAs/NC‐800 exhibits superior activity and excellent reusability for the selective hydrogenation of nitroarenes, surpassing several state‐of‐art non‐noble‐metal catalysts. This study provides a new avenue for the universal fabrication of transition metal SACs.

Single-atom catalysts (SACs) have garnered enormous interest due to their remarkable catalysis activity. However, the exploitation of universal synthesis strategy and regulation of coordination environment of SACs remain a great challenge. Herein, a versatile synthetic strategy is demonstrated to generate a series of transition metal SACs (M SAs/NC, $M=C O$ , Cu, Mn; NC represents the nitrogen-doped carbon) through defect engineering of metalorganic frameworks (MOFs). The interatomic distance between metal sites can be increased by deliberately introducing structural defects within the MOF framework, which inhibits metal aggregation and consequently results in an approximately $70\%$ increase in single metal atom yield. Additionally, the coordination structures of metal sites can also be facilely tuned. The optimized Co SAs/NC-800 exhibits superior activity and excellent reusability for the selective hydrogenation of nitroarenes, surpassing several state-of-art non-noble-metal catalysts. This study provides a new avenue for the universal fabrication of transition metal SACs.
## 1. Introduction
Single-atom catalysts (SACs) with active metal centers dispersed at the atomic level have emerged as a new research frontier for application in catalysis due to their extremely high atom utilization, abundant unsaturated active sites, and tunable electronic properties.[1,2] Combining the advantages of homogeneous and heterogeneous catalysts, SACs usually exhibit ultrahigh catalytic performance and stability in the fields of oxidation,[3–5] reduction,[6,7] and photo- and electro-chemical reactions.[8] To date, tremendous efforts have been devoted to the development of synthetic strategies for SACs, and the current state-of-the-art approaches mainly include atomic layer deposition,[9] wet impregnation,[10] and photochemical reduction,[11] all of which are based on the variation of interaction between metal centers and supports. Despite the great progress achieved in SACs research, the prevention of metal atoms aggregation during the synthetic process remains extremely challenging, severely hindering the further development of highly efficient SACs.[12] In addition, there exist obstacles in the rational regulation of the coordination environment of SACs, which is another crucial determinant for high catalytic performance.
In these regards, the high structural and functional flexibilities of metalorganic frameworks (MOFs) are promising for addressing these challenges.[13] MOFs are a rapidly expanding class of porous crystalline materials comprised of metal ions/clusters and organic ligands.[14] Due to their ultrahigh surface areas, an unprecedented degree of structural diversity, and compositional variability, MOFs are promising materials in which to incorporate catalysts for targeted catalytic reactions.[15] In particular, some MOFs have emerged as excellent candidates for the fabrication of SACs via pyrolysis, owing to their abundant potential coordination sites, high degree of structural tunability, and high porosities.[16] Despite the great progress achieved, the currently available synthetic strategies for obtaining MOFbased SACs are mainly limited to the direct carbonization of the pristine MOF and the expansion of the nearest neighbor metal atom distances by the introduction of a secondary metal in the MOF framework, both of which still confront some inevitable issues.[17] In the former method, metals can easily aggregate into undesirable species such as metal nanoparticles (NPs) or clusters due to MOF’s high density of metal nodes, giving rise to reduced metal single-atom loadings. While the latter approach typically involves making tradeoffs and the removal of introduced secondary metal, and the different complexation properties of dissimilar metal atoms complicate the application of one synthetic method for use with other transition metal atoms, resulting in astriction of the flexibility and universality of this strategy.[18] Therefore, the development of an efficient and versatile synthetic method to increase single-atom loading content is imperative. The chemical variability combined with the structural controllability of MOFs establishes them as opportune candidates for highly loaded SACs. From a MOF design viewpoint, structural defects in MOFs can not only regulate pore properties but they can also determine the density and distribution of active sites, broadening the distances between the targeted active metals and preventing their aggregation during pyrolysis.[19] As a representative, zirconium $(\mathrm{Zr})$ - porphyrinic MOFs, constructed from porphyrin units and highly coordinated $\mathrm{zr}$ clusters, provide nitrogen-rich sites to immobilize transition metals and are advantageous for the rational construction of structural defects.[20] Moreover, their facile synthesis, mesoporosity, and a high tolerance for structural and defect regulation make this class of MOFs a highly desirable platform to efficiently fabricate the SACs.
Herein, we propose a generic strategy for increasing the yield of SACs via defect engineering of MOFs (Scheme 1). Briefly, structural defects were installed into PCN-222(Co) $\begin{array}{r}{[Z\mathrm{r}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{8}(\mathrm{TCPP}.}\end{array}$ $\mathrm{Co})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{8}]$ $\mathrm{\DeltaTCCP=}$ tetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl)porphyrin) by modulator regulation and then subsequently converted into Co SACs upon carbonization and acid etching. The resulting structural defects enhance the yield of metal single-atoms by approximately $70\%$ compared with that of defect-free MOF. By simply varying the pyrolytic temperature, the coordination structure of the Co metal sites can be regulated from $\mathrm{Co-N_{4}C l}$ to ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{4}.$ . The optimal Co SAs/ nitrogen-doped carbon (NC)-800 (NC represents nitrogen-doped carbon) with $\mathrm{Co-N_{4}}$ demonstrates exceptional catalytic performance and excellent stability for the selective hydrogenation of a series of nitroarenes. Moreover, the strategy reported herein exhibits distinct advantages in terms of the compatibility of different metal elements and MOFs with diverse structures. This proof-of-concept study demonstrates that our approach is a promising method for both the general synthesis and performance control of SACs.
## 2. Results and Discussion
A series of defective PCN-222(Co) samples, denoted as DPCN222(Co)-X (X represents the proportion of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in contrast to N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF) during synthesis), were synthesized in the mixture of $10\ \mathrm{mg\ZrCl_{4}}.$ 10  mg Co-TCPP, $250~\mathrm{mg}$ benzoic acid, and different content of water in $2~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF. The concentration of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ has a significant effect on the phase purity and crystallinity of DPCN-222(Co)-X (Figure S2, Supporting Information). When 400 to ${900\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was added to the reaction mixture, the phase-pure PCN-222(Co) in a high yield can be harvested, as evidenced by the powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns. Increasing the amount of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ to $1000~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ , the amorphous products were formed. Field emission scanning electron microscopy (FESEM) images show that the morphology of DPCN-222(Co)-X samples are distinctly different from that of the parent framework (Figure 1e–h; and Figures S3 and S4, Supporting Information). In particular, the surfaces of these samples gradually change from smooth to rough with an increase of water dosage, suggesting the formation of structural defects.[21] The introduction of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ causes competitive coordination with carboxylic acid, thereby promoting crystal nucleation and inhibiting growth, resulting in the formation of structural defects.[22] Variable-temperature PXRD was carried out to evaluate the structural stability of the defect DPCN-222(Co)-X samples. The result shows the DPCN-222(Co)-X samples would not decompose until $350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure S5, Supporting Information). The nitrogen sorption isotherms at 77 K for DPCN-222(Co)-X are presented in Figure S6, Supporting Information. The results reveal that these MOFs feature hierarchical pore structures with pore diameters in the $1.2\mathrm{-}2.9\ \mathrm{nm}$ range (Figure S7, Supporting Information), which facilitate the preparation of porous SACs. It was also found that the Brunauer–Emmett–Teller surface areas of DPCN-222(Co)- X gradually decrease (Table S1, Supporting Information) with the increasing addition of water, due to the excessive defects degrade the crystallinity of the MOF.[23] Thermogravimetric analysis and inductively coupled plasma atomic emission spectroscopy (ICP-AES) measurements were carried out to quantitatively investigate the structure defectivity. In order to obtain the accurate defect degree for different samples, the DPCN-222-X without metal Co samples were prepared. As illustrated in Figure S8, Supporting Information, the normalized weight of DPCN-222-0 at around $365^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ is the expected value $(304\%)$ , and the weight decreases in moving from DPCN-222-0 to DPCN222-45 (from $304\%$ to $260\%$ ), indicating that a portion of the linkers is missing from the framework.[24] ICP-AES analyses confirm that the Co content of the DPCN-222(Co)-X gradually decreases, but the $\mathrm{zr}$ content remains relatively constant (Table S2, Supporting Information). These results verify the presence of structural defects within DPCN-222-X and DPCN$222(\mathrm{Co}){\cdot}\mathrm{X},$ and these defects are a result of intermittent missing linkers. After careful analysis, we found that the linker deficiency gradually increased from DPCN-222(Co)-0 to DPCN$222(\mathrm{Co}){-}45$ (Table S2, Supporting Information). These structural defects may work to expand the spatial distance between Co-TCPP linkers, making the as-obtained MOF a highly desirable platform for the fabrication of SACs.
The as-obtained DPCN-222(Co)-X samples were subjected to annealing at $800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under an $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ atmosphere and subsequently etched by HF. Single Co atoms supported by porous NC (denoted as Co SAs/NC(X)) were obtained. The FESEM and transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images reveal that Co SAs/NC(X) samples inherit the morphology of pristine DPCN-222(Co)-X (Figure S9a–d, Supporting Information), and no evident Co NPs or clusters were observed in the prepared SACs (Figure S9e–h, Supporting Information). Energy dispersive X-ray (EDX) mapping results reveal the C, N, and Co elements homogeneously distributed throughout the entire structure (Figure S9i–l, Supporting Information).
The aberration-corrected high-angle annular dark-field scanning transmission electron microscopy (HAADF-STEM) images exhibit an abundance of bright white-dots on the NC matrix, corresponding to the atomically dispersed Co atoms (Figure 1i–l). These results are in good agreement with PXRD patterns of Co SAs/NC(X), with no diffraction peaks of metal Co. Two broad shoulder peaks in the range of $20{-}25^{\circ}$ and $40{-}45^{\circ}$ , corresponding to the (002) and (101) planes of graphitic carbon (PDF#75-1621), were observed. Furthermore, the sharp peak at $17^{\circ}$ was attributed to the (110) reflections of $\mathrm{C}_{70}$ (PDF#48-1449) (Figure S10, Supporting Information). As determined from ICP-AES measurements, the loading contents of the Co atoms in these samples are 1.00, 0.99, 1.69, and $1.57\mathrm{wt\%}$ for Co SAs/NC(0), Co SAs/NC(25), Co SAs/NC(35), and Co SAs/NC(45), respectively (Table S3, Supporting Information).
Based on the above results, a mechanism illustrating the formation process of Co SAs/NC(X) is presented in Figure  1a–d and Figure 2. First, the periodic arrangement of porphyrin linkers in the MOF ensures a certain degree of isolation between Co active sites. Additionally, the addition of structural defects is more inclined to enlarge the average distance of
Co sites among adjacent Co-TCPP linkers compared with the parent MOF, which is advantageous in mitigating the formation of ${\mathrm{Co}}{-}{\mathrm{Co}}$ bonds at high temperatures. As a result, after carbonization and $\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}$ removal, only $1.00~\mathrm{wt\%}$ of isolated Co atoms were anchored on NC carriers in the defect-free MOF, DPCN-222(Co)-0, due to the relatively short distances between contiguous Co sites. When only a few structural defects are produced as in the case of DPCN-222(Co)-25, the adjacent distance of Co sites is enlarged from d to ${\mathrm{d}}^{\prime}$ , but no significant increase in yield of isolated Co atoms is observed. When the degree of structural defects is further increased, the distances of adjacent Co-TCPP are further expanded from ${\mathrm{d}}^{\prime}$ to $\mathrm{d}^{\prime\prime}$ in DPCN222(Co)-35. In this case, $1.69\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ of atomically dispersed Co atoms is obtained and the loading content of Co single atoms increases by $70\%$ ( $1.69\mathrm{wt\%}$ vs $1.00\ \mathrm{wt}\%$ ) compared with defectfree DPCN-222(Co)-0. When excess structural defects appear, as in the case of DPCN-222(Co)-45, the metal loading drops slightly which is likely due to a lot of metal ions drain from the parent framework. These results suggest that a reasonable number of structural defects can accommodate increasing concentrations of metal single-atoms, rendering the MOF defect engineering strategy promising.
As reported, the catalytic performance of SACs depends on the coordination environments and electronic structures of the active sites.[25] So we tuned the local coordination environment of single-atom Co sites to further reveal the intrinsic relationship between their structures and catalytic properties. Taking DPCN-222(Co)-35 as a representative sample, Co SAs/NC-Y (where Y represents the pyrolysis temperature) with distinct coordination structures were obtained by varying the pyrolysis temperature from 600 to $800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Figures S11–S14, Supporting Information, show the representative FESEM, TEM, and HAADF-STEM images, PXRD patterns, and Raman spectra, which unambiguously demonstrate the absence of visible Co
NPs and clusters in Co SAs/NC-Y. The EDX elemental maps show that Co species are homogeneously distributed over the entire support. The aberration-corrected HAADF-STEM images display large amounts of individual bright dots, indicating the existence of single-atom Co species. Notably, the Co mass percentages in Co SAs/NC-600, Co SAs/NC-700, and Co SAs/ NC-800 are 3.00, 2.72, and $1.69~\mathrm{wt\%}$ based on ICP-AES measurements, respectively (Table S4, Supporting Information). In addition, the $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ sorption isotherms of Co SAs/NC-Y present typical type IV isotherms, indicating the presence of hierarchically porous structures (Figure S15, Supporting Information). All samples possess not only high surface areas of 507, 569, and $603~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , but also corroborate the presence of hierarchically porous structures composed of micropores and mesopores (Table S4, Supporting Information), which favor exposure of the abundant active sites and promote mass transfer that is essential to enhance catalytic activity.
For an in-depth investigation of the microstructural and electronic properties of single-atom Co sites, X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) and X-ray absorption spectro­ scopy techniques were performed. As shown in Figure 3a and Figure S16, Supporting Information, the N 1s of the Co SAs/ NC-Y can be deconvoluted into five peaks at around 398.5, 399.2, 400.3, 401.2, and $402.7\ \mathrm{eV},$ corresponding to pyridinic N, Co–N, pyrrolic N, graphitic N, and oxidized N, respectively. The Co 2p spectrum of Co SAs/NC-800 shows two peaks with binding energies of 780.4 and $795.8\ \mathrm{eV},$ suggesting the presence of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ species. Two new signal peaks at 781.9 and 797.1  eV assigned to ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ were observed in Co SAs/NC-600 and Co SAs/NC-700 (Figure  3b), respectively.[26] In the Co K-edge X-ray absorption near-edge structure (XANES) spectra (Figure 3c), the absorption edges of all Co SAs/NC-Y samples are located between those of CoO and $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ , indicating that the positive valence state of the Co atoms is situated between $+2$ and $+3.^{[27\mathrm{a}]}$ Moreover, Co SAs/NC-800’s $\mathrm{E}_{0}$ value of $7710.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ (the first inflection point) lies closest to that of the CoO reference, implying the presence of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ . By contrast, both Co SAs/NC-600 and Co SAs/NC-700 have an $\mathrm{E}_{0}$ value of $7709.9{\mathrm{~eV}},$ which is better approximated by the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ reference value, indicating the joint presence of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ and ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ .[27b] These results are in agreement with the XPS results. In the Fourier-transformed extended X-ray absorption fine structure (FT-EXAFS) spectra, all of the Co SAs/NC-Y samples show a major peak at 1.33–1.42 Å, attributed to the first Co–N coordination shell[27c] (Figure  3d and Figure S17, Supporting Information). The fact that no peak related to the $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Co-Co}$ bond at 2.16 Å was observed for these samples, combined with the wavelet transform (WT) EXAFS plots (Figure 3g) further verified the isolation of Co single atoms in Co SAs/NC-Y samples. Importantly, a distinct shoulder at $1.96\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ appears in both the Co SAs/NC-600 and Co SAs/NC-700 spectra that matches the CoCl bond length,[28] while this shoulder is absent for Co SAs/NC-800. This result indicates that Co is stabilized by the Co–N and CoCl bonds in both Co SAs/NC-600 and Co SAs/NC-700, consistent with the XPS results (Figure S18,
Supporting Information). EXAFS fitting was then carried out to identify the quantitative coordination environment of the Co atoms in Co SAs/NC-Y samples. The fitting curves suggest that the isolated Co atoms are coordinated to four N atoms, stabilized in the form of ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{4}$ in Co SAs/NC-800 (Figure 3e, Table S5, Supporting Information). Meanwhile, four N atoms and one Cl atom are coordinated to each Co center (denoted as $\mathrm{Co-N_{4}C l}$ ) in Co SAs/NC-600 and Co SAs/NC-700, respectively (Figure  3f and Figure S19, Table S5, Supporting Information).
To explore the potential applications of Co SACs, the selective hydrogenation of nitroarenes at different conditions (temperature, hydrogen pressure, solvent, and reaction time) was investigated (Figure 4a–c and Figure S20, Table S6, Supporting Information). As shown in Figure 4a, it was found that Co SAs/ NC-800 affords a high yield of aniline $(99.8\%)$ in aqueous solution under 5 bar $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ with a turnover frequency (TOF) of $18.2\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ . By comparison, both Co SAs/NC-600 and Co SAs/NC-700 exhibit much lower yields of $33.8\%$ and $71.5\%$ under the same conditions with TOF of only 6.1 and $13.1\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ , respectively. These results demonstrate that the differences in performance are intimately related to the distinct electronic properties and coordination structures of the Co atoms. Notably, Co SAs/NC-800 delivers higher catalytic activity than many non-precious metal catalysts reported, with a TOF of more than $109.1\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ at 30 bar (Figure  4e and Table S7, Supporting Information). Control experiments using NC-800 and $\mathrm{ZrO_{2}/N C{-}800}$ derived from PCN-222 without the Co sites fail to drive the reaction forward under the same conditions, implying that the excellent hydrogenation activity of Co SAs/NC-800 is ascribed mainly to $\mathrm{Co-N}$ sites rather than C-N or $\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}$ sites. For comparison, the Co NPs embedded on NC (Co NPs/NC-800) were prepared. TEM and EDX mapping images show that Co NPs $\mathrm{5-10~nm}$ ) are uniformly dispersed on the NC carriers (Figure S21, Supporting Information). The hydrogenation performance of Co NPs/NC-800 was also measured under the same condition. It is found that Co NPs alone only account for a yield of $44.7\%$ . Last, $\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}$ -Co SAs/NC-800 also shows an insufficient $75.3\%$ yield (Figure  S22, Supporting Information). Altogether, these illustrate the vital contribution of atomically dispersed Co atoms and the large pore structures within SACs. Beyond the remarkable catalytic performance, the Co SAs/NC-800 catalyst also shows high stability and no significant decrease in conversion efficiency or selectivity after five cycles (Figure  4d). Moreover, the high-resolution-TEM, HAADF-STEM, PXRD, and XPS characterizations (Figures  S23 and S24, Supporting Information) show that the isolated Co sites are not damaged following cycling tests. Above results demonstrate the Co SAs/NC-800 exhibits outstanding stability. Remarkably, the Co SAs/NC-800 also exhibits excellent performance for the selective hydrogenation of a broad scope of nitroarenes with different substituents including electron-donating groups, electron-withdrawing groups, halogen, aldehydes, ketones, esters, heterocyclic- and phenyl-, nearly $90.2\mathrm{-}99.8\%$ yields of the corresponding products were achieved under mild conditions (Figure S25, Supporting Information). The high catalytic activities of Co SAs/NC-800 may be attributed to its atomically dispersed metal sites and large porous structure. These results imply the generic applicability of Co SAs/NC-800 for efficient selective hydrogenation of nitroarenes.
To understand the mechanism of high hydrogenation activity of the Co SAs/NC-800 catalyst, density functional theory calculations were performed. The Bader charge analysis suggests the charge of single-atom Co in Co SAs/NC-800 is $+0.825$ |e|, which is lower than that of Co SAs/NC-600 (+1.211  |e|), indicating that the higher electron density of Co single atoms in the Co SAs/NC-800 (Figure S26, Supporting Information). This result is in accordance with the XPS and XANES spectra. The increase in electron density was more favorable for the denotation of electrons from metal Co to $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , facilitating the dissociation of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules.[29] Furthermore, the reaction energy of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ dissociation reveals that the Co SAs/NC-800 catalyst not only decreases the barrier from 1.14 to $0.56\mathrm{eV},$ but also reduces the reaction energy of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ cleavage from $-1.35$ to $-1.82\ \mathrm{eV}$ compared with Co SAs/NC-600 (Figure 4f). Therefore, the Co SAs/ NC-800 is more advantageous for the nitrobenzene hydrogenation reaction.
To prove the general applicability of MOF defect engineering strategy, we extended this synthetic strategy to other MOF systems. Replacing DPCN-222(Co) precursors with either DPCN-222(Cu) or DPCN-222(Mn) (Figure S27, Supporting Information), Cu SAs/NC and Mn SAs/NC were successfully prepared (Figure 5a–h and Figure S28, Supporting Information). The corresponding aberration-corrected HAADFSTEM confirms that isolated Cu or Mn atoms were uniformly dispersed across the NC supports. Moreover, other defective MOFs with diverse structures, such as defective PCN-223(Co), consisting of $\ensuremath{\mathrm{zr}}_{6}$ nodes that are 12-connected instead of 8-connected as in DPCN-222(Co), were also prepared for the synthesis of SACs with this synthetic strategy. The as-synthesized DPCN-223(Co) shows a PXRD pattern similar to defect-free PCN-223(Co), suggesting relatively maintained crystallinity (Figure S29, Supporting Information). As expected, single Co atoms were successfully dispersed after carbonization and oxide removal by acid etching. The TEM and PXRD characterizations (Figure  5i and Figure S30, Supporting Information) corroborate that no metal NPs or clusters appeared. The EDX mapping images (Figure 5j) demonstrate the existence of homogenously dispersed Co and N elements on the entire NC support. The aberration-corrected HAADF-TEM images (Figure  5k,l) show many isolated bright spots, further confirming that the Co atoms exist in the form of single-atom sites. Interestingly, ICP-AES analyses reveal that metal loadings obtained by defect MOFs are twice as high as that of the corresponding defect-free MOFs (Table S3, entry 5–7, Supporting Information). These studies highlight the universality of our strategy for the preparation of SACs.
## 3. Conclusion
In summary, we have presented a versatile method to prepare a series of transition metal SACs via an innovative MOF defect engineering strategy. Optimization of the degree of structural defects brings about an increase in spatial distance between adjacent metal atoms in the MOF skeleton, conducive to the formation of single metal atoms upon pyrolysis. Profiting from the periodicity and tunability of MOFs, controlling the spatial distance between adjacent metal centers within porphyrinic MOFs attenuates unwanted aggregation during pyrolysis, giving rise to a nearly $70\%$ increase in the yield of single metal atoms. Additionally, the coordination environment of the metal single atoms could be facilely modified by varying the pyrolytic temperature. Due to the atomically dispersed Co sites, high surface areas, hierarchically porous structure, and unique electron configuration, the resultant Co SAs/NC-800 catalyst exhibits excellent activity for selective hydrogenation of various nitroarenes in an aqueous solution. The concepts and approaches presented by this work open new avenues for further design of transition metal SACs with high catalytic activity.
